# BMM standalone repo server

Generated by BMM ({{EDITION}}). It serves this folder's `repo.json` and mod files to BMM users,
without BMM running. Port: **{{PORT}}**.

## Start it

| Where | How |
|---|---|
| Windows | {{START_WINDOWS}} |
| Linux / macOS | {{START_LINUX}} |
| Docker | `docker compose up -d` (when BMM generated a `Dockerfile`) |
| At boot, Linux | `bmm-server/bmm-repo.service` (systemd, see below) |
| At boot, Windows | `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File bmm-server\windows-task.ps1` (as administrator) |

Then open `http://127.0.0.1:{{PORT}}/status`.

## Addresses

| Path | What | Who |
|---|---|---|
| `/repo.json` | The manifest. ETag + `304 Not Modified` for an unchanged repo, gzip | Subscribers (password / keys if set) |
| `/mods/...` | Mod files. `ETag`, `Last-Modified`, `304`, `Range` (resume), `HEAD` | Subscribers |
| `/feed.xml`, `/feed.atom` | Atom feed: one entry per mod version | Subscribers |
| `/status` | Status page: repo, version, format, files, size, last update, feed, scheduled tasks, « Open in BMM » | Anyone. A protected repo shows its details only with `?password=` |
| `/status.json` | The same, as JSON | Anyone (same rule) |
| `/health` | `200 {"ok":true}` while `repo.json` is readable, `503` otherwise | Anyone (Docker, systemd, uptime monitors) |
| `/monitoring.json` | Totals only, no IP address, no Creator ID | Anyone |
| `/dashboard`, `/admin/*` | Admin panel (where the edition has one) | Admin password |
| `/admin/jobs`, `POST /admin/jobs/run?id=<job>` | Job state; run a job now | Admin password (`Authorization` header) |

Never served: every file at the root except `repo.json` / `Info.json`, `node_modules/`, and
`bmm-server/` (job state, backups).

## Add it in BMM

Server Repo → Connect, paste `http://<this-host>:{{PORT}}/`, or click **Open in BMM** on the status
page (`bmm://repo/connect?url=…`).

## Protecting the repo: `access.json`

```json
{ "password": "", "pubkeys": [], "audience": "" }
```

- `password`: subscribers send it as `X-Repo-Password` (or `?password=`). Blank = open.
- `pubkeys`: one-line OpenSSH public keys; listing one makes a signed proof required.
- `audience`: the address subscribers type (`https://repo.example.com`), needed with keys.

Read on every request: edit it, no restart. `bans.json` and `whitelist.json` work the same way.

## Configuration: `server.config.json`

Re-read when it changes. Environment variables win over it:

| Variable | Effect |
|---|---|
| `PORT` | Listening port (default {{PORT}}) |
| `ADMIN_PASSWORD` | Admin password (editions with an admin panel) |
| `BMM_PUBLIC_URL` | The public address, for the feed and the « Open in BMM » link |
| `BMM_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` | The key-proof audience (same as `audience` in access.json) |
| `BMM_RATE_LIMIT` | Requests per minute per address, `0` = off |
| `BMM_JOBS=off` | No scheduled run (jobs can still be started from `/admin/jobs`) |
| `BMM_STATUS_PAGE=off` | No `/status` |
| `BMM_CONFIG` | Another config file |

`rateLimit`: `{ "perMinute": 600, "burst": 300 }` answers `429` + `Retry-After` past the burst.
Requests from this machine are never limited. A sync downloads one file per request: keep the
limit well above the number of files a subscriber pulls at once.

## Scheduled tasks

Configured now:

{{JOBS}}

Each entry of `jobs` has an `id`, a `type`, and **either** `"every": "15m"` (`s`, `m`, `h`, `d`,
`w`) **or** `"cron": "0 3 * * *"` (minute hour day month weekday, local time; `*`, `*/n`, `a-b`,
`a,b`, `@hourly`, `@daily`, `@weekly`, `@monthly`). `"enabled": false` pauses it, `"runOnStart":
true` also runs it at start. A run missed while the server was off happens once at the next start.
Last run, result and next run are on `/status`; every run is also logged in `server.log`.

| `type` | What it does | Options |
|---|---|---|
| `rescan` | Checks every file `repo.json` lists against the disk (size, SHA-256), counts missing files and files not in the manifest | `rewrite` (update the hashes in an **unsigned** manifest; a signed one is never rewritten, re-export it from BMM), `bumpVersion`, `fullHash` |
| `sync` | Updates the repo from a source, then optionally rescans | `source.kind`: `git` (`git pull --ff-only`), `rsync` (`source.path`, `source.delete`), `folder` (a folder BMM exports into: `source.path`, `source.include`, `source.mirror`), `command` (`source.command`: an argument array, no shell); `thenRescan`, `timeoutMs` |
| `backup` | Copies `repo.json` to `bmm-server/backups/` (skipped when unchanged) | `keep` (default 14) |
| `rotateLogs` | Rotates `server.log` past a size | `maxSizeMB` (10), `keep` (5) |

A sync never overwrites the server's own files (`access.json`, `server.config.json`, the
scripts, `bmm-server/`…). Before a rescan or a sync rewrites `repo.json`, a copy goes to
`bmm-server/backups/`.

## HTTPS

Put the server behind a reverse proxy that holds the certificate, and set `publicUrl` (or
`BMM_PUBLIC_URL`) to the `https://` address.

Caddy (automatic certificate):

```
repo.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{{PORT}}
}
```

nginx:

```
location / {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:{{PORT}};
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_buffering off;
}
```

The script editions (v1, v2) read `X-Forwarded-For` for the caller's address, so expose it only through the proxy
(firewall the port), or a client can claim any address.

## Start at boot

**systemd** (Linux): copy the folder to `/srv/bmm-repo` (or edit `WorkingDirectory`), then

```
sudo cp bmm-server/bmm-repo.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now bmm-repo
journalctl -u bmm-repo -f
```

**Windows**: `bmm-server\windows-task.ps1` registers a scheduled task that starts the server at
boot (`-Remove` deletes it).

## Self-test

```
node bmm-server/selftest.js
```

Checks the schedules, ranges, caching, feed, status page and jobs on a temporary copy; your repo
is not touched. Exit code 0 = all passed.
